Third-party programs bundled with the desktop apps
--------------------------------------------------
The SecuraCV Flasher and SecuraCV Lab installers bundle espflash, the flash
engine, as a separate program. It is licensed under the MIT License or the
Apache License, Version 2.0, at your option:
  Copyright (c) 2022 The Espflash Project Developers
  https://github.com/esp-rs/espflash
The SecuraCV Flasher installers also bundle two things from Raspberry Pi's
usbboot:
  https://github.com/raspberrypi/usbboot
rpiboot, the program that runs on your computer, is licensed under the Apache
License, Version 2.0 (usbboot's LICENSE file). The mass-storage-gadget64 boot
image that rpiboot sends to the board is not one program under that one
license. usbboot's own mass-storage-gadget64/README.md calls it a bootloader
image that loads a Linux initramfs, so it is a Linux system image whose
components carry their own licenses, the Linux kernel's GNU General Public
License, version 2, among them. usbboot names its source as the
mass-storage-gadget64 branch of
  https://github.com/raspberrypi/buildroot
The macOS Flasher installers also bundle libusb as a separate dynamic library,
licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later:
  https://github.com/libusb/libusb
The versions bundled are the ones pinned in .github/espflash-pins.env and in
.github/workflows/desktop-flasher-release.yml. Each Flasher and Lab release
built by those workflows attaches, as THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.html, the full
license texts of espflash, rpiboot and libusb and of every Rust crate compiled
into the app. The license texts of the boot image's components are not
reproduced there; the file names the image and points at its source.
